{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.SeparableInterfaceHypothesesHaveContent","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.SeparableInterfaceHypothesesHaveContent","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.SeparableInterfaceContent.asymmetry_attainable_with_distinct_scalars","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.SeparableInterfaceContent.asymmetry_attainable_with_distinct_scalars","kind":"theorem","line":186,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SeparableInterfaceHypothesesHaveContent.lean#L186-L199","doc":"**Distinct scalars reach every ratio.**\n\nIf the spin `SU(2)` carries `(μ₊, μ₋)` and the isospin `SU(2)` carries a *different*\npair `(ν₊, ν₋)`, the channel ratio `r` defined by `μ₊ ν₋ = r · (μ₋ ν₊)` is unconstrained:\nfor every `r` there is such a pair. So no bound on the achievable asymmetry survives the\nloss of `hplus`/`hminus` — not a weakened bound, none at all.\n\nScope (b) on the conjunction {separable interface, distinct scalar pairs}, invariant\nnamed: a field has no nontrivial multiplicative constraint between two free elements. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.SeparableInterfaceContent.degeneracy_is_commutativity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.SeparableInterfaceContent.degeneracy_is_commutativity","kind":"theorem","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SeparableInterfaceHypothesesHaveContent.lean#L175-L182","doc":"**What `separable_interface_degenerate` proves, once its antecedent is applied.**\n\nRewriting by `hplus` and `hminus` replaces both spin scalars by isospin ones, and the\ntwo sides then differ by the order of a product. So the closure's mathematical content\nis `mul_comm`; its physical content is entirely in the identification the hypotheses\nmake. Stated here separately so that this is visible without reading a proof term. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.SeparableInterfaceContent.separability_hypotheses_have_content","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.SeparableInterfaceContent.separability_hypotheses_have_content","kind":"theorem","line":154,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SeparableInterfaceHypothesesHaveContent.lean#L154-L171","doc":"**The separability conclusion is false without `hplus`/`hminus`.**\n\nIndex the two channels by `Bool`. With `fSpin = (2, 1)`, `fIso = (5, 3)` (reading\n`true ↦` the first slot) and a unit space factor, the two sides are `5` and `6`.\n\nThe only thing the ring must supply is that those two are different — carried as the\nhypothesis `(5 : R) ≠ 6` rather than deduced from an ordering, so the statement stays on\nthe generic side of the §7c wall. Its `R := ℝ` instance is in the sibling file.\n\nThis is the `gate_has_content` pattern this arc uses elsewhere: a theorem whose\nhypotheses could be dropped without changing the conclusion asserts nothing, and the\ncheapest way to know which kind you have is to exhibit a failure. ","signatureChunk":0}]}